# Configuration reload

Every built application exposes a cloneable `RuntimeReloader`. A reload re-reads the manager's retained sources in their original merge order, rebinds changed typed values, rebuilds the components that depend on them in a candidate generation, runs affected hooks against it, and publishes the candidate only if every stage succeeds. The same pipeline serves manual requests, `SIGHUP` and file watching.

## Enable the required surface

Manual reload is always available. File watching requires the facade's `watch` feature; SIGHUP is a native Unix trigger.

```toml
[dependencies]
upwell = { version = "1", features = ["watch"] }
```

Automatic setup belongs to <Symbol path="upwell_config::managed::source::ConfigManager" />, not to a standalone watcher:

```rust
use std::time::Duration;
use upwell::prelude::*;

let config = ConfigManager::load_from(&directories, &profiles)?
    .watch_config()
    .reload_on_sighup()
    .config_reload_debounce(Duration::from_millis(500));

let app = App::builder("catalog-api")
    .config_source(config)
    .auto_discover()
    .build()
    .await?;
```

`App::run` and `App::serve` read those manager-owned triggers after startup succeeds, spawn the tasks, and stop them before shutdown hooks run. Without `watch`, `watch_config()` is logged and ignored at startup rather than making file watching available.

## Reload manually

<Example title="Use the application-owned reloader">

```rust
let reloader = app.reloader();
let report = reloader.reload().await?;

tracing::info!(
    cause = %report.cause(),
    committed = report.is_committed(),
    config_generation = report.config_generation(),
    changed = report.changed().len(),
    "reload finished"
);
```

`reload()` returns `Result<RuntimeTransitionReport, upwell::AppError>`. A source change that commits reports `is_committed()` with the new `runtime_generation()` and `config_generation()`. An unchanged source is a no-op: `Ok` with `TransitionOutcome::NoOp`, no hooks, and no generation movement. The report lists changed binding paths and type names, never values, so it is safe to log.

</Example>

`App::reloader()` is the public handle. The old `app.config_reloader()` is not public API.

The handle is also injectable, so a component can request a reload without holding the `App`. Use an authenticated administrative route or a deployment control task when the platform has no filesystem notification or signals.

```rust
use upwell::{RuntimeReloader, component};

#[component]
struct ReloadEndpoint {
    reloader: RuntimeReloader,
}

impl ReloadEndpoint {
    async fn refresh(&self) -> Result<(), upwell::AppError> {
        self.reloader.reload().await?;

        Ok(())
    }
}
```

`reload()` fails with `RuntimeUnavailable` if the handle is used while the application is still being built or after its runtime was dropped, and with `ReentrantReload` when called from inside a running reload (see [Troubleshoot reloads](#troubleshoot-reloads)).

<Callout type="info" title="Runtime transitions">

`ConfigReloader` reports the config-level result. `AppRuntime::reload_config()` and `RuntimeReloader::reload()` run the full runtime transition, including proposal, decision, and retirement hooks, and return a `RuntimeTransitionReport`. See <GuideRef slug="framework/application/transition-hooks">runtime transition hooks</GuideRef>.

</Callout>

## Validate a proposal with `CfgNext`

<Example title="Reject an unusable pool configuration">

```rust
#[component]
struct DatabasePool;

#[methods]
impl DatabasePool {
    #[hook(ConfigReload)]
    async fn reload(
        &self,
        next: CfgNext<DatabaseConfig>,
    ) -> Result<HookOutcome, PoolError> {
        if next.get().pool_size == 0 {
            return Err(PoolError::EmptyPool);
        }

        self.prepare_pool(next.snapshot()).await?;

        Ok(HookOutcome::Reloaded)
    }
}
```

`ConfigReload` participants run sequentially in registration order, not concurrently, and the first `Err` aborts the reload: later participants and observers do not run. A hook that panics is caught and reported as `HookPanicked` without the panic payload. Hooks run against the candidate generation, after it is constructed and before it is published.

<Symbol path="upwell_config::managed::CfgNext" /> is read-only and exposes the candidate value, its binding path, and an owned snapshot. The proposal contains staged values for every binding, including unchanged bindings, so a hook with several <code>CfgNext&lt;T&gt;</code> parameters sees one coherent candidate generation.

</Example>

## Understand the transaction

A reload is a serialized config-and-graph transition. The config reloader's lock is taken first, then the runtime transition writer, and both are held until the attempt ends. The phases, as reported by `TransitionPhase`, are:

1. `Stage`: re-read every retained source from an empty tree in original precedence order, seed defaults, and compare each committed binding's raw subtree and consumed placeholder dependencies. An unchanged source ends here as a no-op.
2. `Evaluate`: re-evaluate component conditions against the staged values.
3. `Plan`: derive the candidate component graph and decide which components are retained, replaced, rebound, added or removed.
4. `Proposal`: run `TransitionProposal` hooks against the active generation. Nothing is constructed yet.
5. `Construct`: build the candidate root over the candidate config. Only affected singletons are rebuilt; unaffected ones keep their instances.
6. `Hooks`: run affected `ConfigReload` hooks sequentially against the candidate.
7. `Decision`: run `TransitionDecision` hooks against the candidate, immediately before commit.
8. `Commit`: publish the candidate as the runtime generation and commit the live config slots. The config generation advances exactly once.

After the locks are released, displaced singletons receive `ComponentRetired`, then `TransitionCommitted` or `TransitionAborted` observers run. These cannot roll anything back.

A rejection in phases 1 to 7 leaves the active generation, the managed config tree, its live slots and the config generation untouched, and drops the candidate. The caller receives `AppError::TransitionRejected`, which carries the redacted report: the rejecting `TransitionPhase` and a `TransitionFailure` category. `rejection.error()` returns the original error.

Side effects outside the framework are not rolled back. A hook that performs external work must clean up anything it prepared before returning `Err`; the framework can discard a candidate generation and keep live config slots, not an arbitrary database connection created by user code. Mutations made through retained instances that the candidate aliases also persist.

Proposal and decision hooks, and how each affected component keeps or replaces its instance, are covered by the transition hooks and transition strategies guides.

## Debounce and platform behavior

The default debounce is 250 ms. It coalesces bursts of watcher events; it does not make file replacement atomic. Write deployment config through an atomic rename where possible and rely on transactional rejection for partial or malformed reads.

The trigger decides the report's `ReloadCause`: `Requested` for `RuntimeReloader::reload`, `Signal` for `SIGHUP`, and `FileWatch` for a watched source file. All three run the same transaction and fire the same hooks.

SIGHUP is Unix-only. File watching is a native filesystem facility. Browser Wasm has neither automatic trigger, but code may still call a server endpoint that performs a manual reload in the native host.

<Callout type="warning" title="A notification is not a commit">

Watcher delivery means a source may have changed. Observe the reload result or generation before assuming live values moved. Rejected candidates preserve the previous generation.

</Callout>

## Troubleshoot reloads

A rejected reload returns `AppError::TransitionRejected`. Read `rejection.report().outcome()` for the phase and `TransitionFailure`, and `rejection.error()` for the underlying error.

- `ConfigLoad` or `ConfigBind`: a retained source disappeared, became unreadable or no longer parses, or a changed subtree or placeholder cannot deserialize into its registered type. `ConfigBind` names the path and type.
- `HookRejected` names the component whose hook rejected the attempt. The underlying error is `ConfigReloadError::Hook` for a `ConfigReload` hook, `TransitionHookRejected` when a proposal or decision hook returned `Reject` with its reason code, or `TransitionHookFailed` when such a hook returned an error.
- `HookPanicked` means a participant hook panicked. Panic payloads are omitted because they may contain secrets.
- `Panicked` protects the process from a panic in user-provided deserialization, a condition callback or a factory.
- `RestartRequired` means an affected component cannot be reconstructed in place; the report names the component and reason, and nothing was constructed.
- `ReentrantReload` means a `ConfigReload`, proposal or decision hook, or a candidate factory, called `reload()` for its own application. The running attempt holds non-reentrant locks, so the nested call fails immediately and the outer reload is unaffected. Request the follow-up from a `TransitionCommitted` or `TransitionAborted` observer, which run after the locks are released.
- `RuntimeUnavailable` means the reloader was used before the application was built or after its runtime was dropped. It is returned directly, not wrapped in a report.
- No file event usually means `watch` was not compiled, the manager did not request `watch_config`, or the application has not entered `run`/`serve`.
- A changed environment variable can trigger a new binding value on manual reload even when the source subtree is unchanged, but only placeholders consumed by the prior typed read participate in that dependency check.

See <GuideRef slug="framework/configuration/sources-and-bindings">configuration sources</GuideRef> and <GuideRef slug="framework/application/lifecycle">lifecycle and shutdown</GuideRef>.

To predict whether components keep, rebind or rebuild their instances when a reload changes configuration, see <GuideRef slug="framework/dependency-injection/transition-strategies">Component transition strategies</GuideRef>.
